<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lachine massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lachine massacre was a surprise attack by about 1,500 Iroquois warriors on the French settlement of Lachine, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, on 5 August 1689. Dozens of settlers were killed and many more taken captive, and the raid spread terror across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. It was a reprisal for the campaign of the governor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-rene-de-brisay-de-denonville">Marquis de Denonville</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> in 1687, and it opened a decade of war between New France and the Iroquois.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lachine-massacre.jpg" alt="Lachine massacre" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P.L. Morin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_of_Montreal,_1687-1723.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Relations between the French and the Haudenosaunee had worsened through the 17th century with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> and with French missions that asked converts to give up native traditions. In 1666 expeditions under Courcelles and Tracy burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> villages and much of their winter corn, and Denonville's invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> country in 1687 destroyed about 1,200,000 bushels of corn. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> went to war in 1689, English colonists in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> encouraged the Haudenosaunee to strike the scattered and poorly defended settlements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, a raid also shaped by the tradition of mourning wars, fought to avenge wrongs and to take captives to replace the dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the rainy morning of 5 August 1689 the warriors crossed Lake Saint-Louis by boat and landed on the south shore of the Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> while the 375 inhabitants of Lachine slept. At a signal they broke into the houses and dragged people outside, and where settlers barricaded themselves they set the buildings on fire. A survivor reached a garrison three miles away, and the French sent 200 soldiers under Daniel d'Auger de Subercase with 100 armed civilians, who protected some of the fleeing colonists, but Governor Denonville, seeking a diplomatic course, recalled them to Fort Rolland though he had 700 soldiers in Montreal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The first reports greatly inflated the death toll; the figure of 24 dead, with more than 70 taken prisoner, comes from Colby's study of the Catholic parish registers. French sources said only three attackers were killed. Surviving captives told of companions tortured, burned and eaten, and the Sulpician superior François Vachon de Belmont described such scenes, though since every written account comes from the French side some of these reports may be exaggerated; no Haudenosaunee account survives in writing. Firearms from the English found on the island fed French hostility towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Denonville was recalled, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-de-buade-de-frontenac">Louis de Buade de Frontenac</a> answered with winter raids on the English colonies in 1690, among them the Schenectady massacre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lachine massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Marquis de Tracy burned Mohawk villages in 1666, the Marquis de Denonville invaded Seneca country in 1687, and Frontenac struck the Onondaga in 1696, while Haudenosaunee warriors raided <b>Lachine</b> near Montreal in 1689.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
